. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H4 * 
. 
.         * having daughters --> stronger support for gender equality (attitude
> s), more for men
.         * OLS regression
.         * interaction between having daughter and being male 
.         * robust standard errors to account for heteroskedasticity 
.                 * (possibility that error term is not constant across respond
> ents)
. /*      
>         reg genderatt_1618 daughter##male                                    
>                                                    ///
>                 i.gebjahr c.log_netincome i.pgisced97_mi i.pgfamstd_mi       
>                            ///
>                 if syear == 2016 | syear == 2017 | syear == 2018 & pid_count 
> == 1,              ///
>                 vce(robust)
> 
>                 note:
>                 the N of the 5% sample is too small to run the above regressi
> on. in the 
>                 following, we omit the control for income, to make the regres
> sion work
>                 on the 5% sample. if you run the code on the full sample, ple
> ase try
>                 the above regression code
> */
. 
.         reg genderatt_1618 daughter##male                                    
>                                                    ///
>                 i.gebjahr i.pgisced97_mi i.pgfamstd_mi                       
>                                            ///
>                 if syear == 2016 | syear == 2017 | syear == 2018 & pid_count 
> == 1,              ///
>                 vce(robust)
note: 1.pgfamstd_mi omitted because of collinearity.

